Whenever a monster is generated that is:

- stationary (i.e. asleep, as in a treasure zoo or throne room)
- female
- egg-laying and nesting,

there should be a chance that the monster is generated sitting on top
of a nest. The nest would contain ~5 eggs of whatever monster type the
mother is. It would also have a chance at containing some food or shiny
trinkets.

A nesting creature will never leave the nest, not even to flee (or
attack); if teleported away from the nest she will make her way back to
it immediately. Because she is guarding her nestlings, she will attack
more viciously than a regular monster of her type and will fight to the
last hitpoint rather than flee; she would also be far less susceptible
to scrolls/spells of taming. There should also be a chance that her
mate is waiting nearby, to attack anyone who approaches.

Essentially, this would make dragons/winged gargoyles a lot more
dangerous for the early-game adventurer. On the other hand, if your
character manages to survive, you've got a nice litter of, say, five
baby silver dragons waiting for you a few hundred turns down the road.

